ViewBuilder
@ViewBuilder is one of the most important concepts in SwiftUI for iOS development, especially in interviews. It is a result builder that enables a declarative, DSL-like syntax for composing multiple views inside a closure without explicit return statements or grouping.

It transforms multiple view expressions in a closure into a single View (often a TupleView or Group under the hood).

	•	Used extensively in containers like VStack, HStack, List, Button (label), custom views, etc.

import SwiftUI
struct ContentView: View {
    var body: some View {           // Implicitly uses @ViewBuilder
        VStack {                    // @ViewBuilder in action
            Text("Hello")
            Text("World")
            Image(systemName: "star")
            Rectangle().fill(.blue).frame(height: 50)
        }
    }
}
You don't need return or to wrap everything in a Group.
===
Group is a container view in SwiftUI that groups multiple views together without adding any visual styling, layout, or extra view hierarchy overhead.

It is transparent — it doesn't affect layout, spacing, background, or appearance. It only serves as a logical container.

Common Use Cases
	0.	Applying modifiers to multiple views at once
Swift


Group {
    Text("Hello")
    Text("World")
    Text("SwiftUI")
}
.font(.title)
.foregroundColor(.blue)
.padding()

	0.	Conditional Views (with @ViewBuilder)
Swift


@ViewBuilder
var content: some View {
    if showAdvanced {
        Group {
            Text("Advanced Feature 1")
            Text("Advanced Feature 2")
            Slider(value: $value)
        }
    } else {
        Text("Basic Mode")
    }
}

What are Opaque Types in Swift?
"Opaque types, introduced using the some keyword, allow us to hide the concrete implementation type while exposing only the protocol it conforms to. This provides abstraction without losing compile-time type information, so we get both flexibility and performance. SwiftUI heavily relies on opaque types through some View."


What is the difference between some and any?
Answer: "some represents a single hidden concrete type chosen by the implementation, whereas any represents a type-erased protocol that can hold any conforming type at runtime. Because the compiler knows the underlying type for some, it generally offers better performance than any.


Why does SwiftUI use some View instead of View?
Answer: "The View protocol contains an associated type, so it cannot be used directly as a return type. By using some View, SwiftUI hides the complex underlying view hierarchy while preserving type safety and compile-time optimizations. This keeps the API simple while maintaining performance."

Can a function returning some Protocol return different concrete types?
Answer: "No. A function returning an opaque type must always return the same underlying concrete type across all code paths. If multiple concrete types are needed, we typically use type erasure with any or a custom wrapper."

Give a real-world SwiftUI use case for Opaque Types.
Answer: "Every SwiftUI view uses opaque types through var body: some View. This allows SwiftUI to hide the extremely complex types created by modifier chains while still enabling compile-time checking and efficient view diffing."



