Binding creates a two-way connection between a view and its data source, ensuring the view updates when data changes and vice versa.

Whenever there is a change in datasource it gets updated automatically in view and vice versa.
Data binding can be achieved using  property wrappers like state, binding. StateObject and environment object.

@State: Manages local, mutable state within a view. Use for simple data like toggles or text input.
@Binding: Allows a child view to read and write to a parent’s @State or other data source.
@StateObject: Manages an ObservableObject’s lifecycle, typically used for complex data models.
@ObservedObject: Subscribes to an ObservableObject for updates but doesn’t own the object.
@Published: Marks properties in an ObservableObject to trigger view updates when changed.

Stateobject is a property wrapper used to hold reference type which confirm to observable object protocal.

This is used to create and managethe instance of a class that conforms to the observable object protocal.

Observable object protocal monitors the changes in the published property and send an event(object will change) to the subscriber.

This object is created only once and preserved across view reloads.


@StateObject is a property wrapper used in SwiftUI to create and own an instance of a reference type that conforms to the ObservableObject protocol.
It is designed to initialize, store, and manage the lifecycle of the object once for the lifetime of the view. Unlike @ObservedObject, @StateObject ensures the object is created once and preserved across view reloads (e.g., when the view body re-renders).
The ObservableObject protocol allows the class to publish changes through @Published properties. When an @Published property changes, the object automatically sends a objectWillChange event, notifying any views or subscribers that are observing it to update the UI.


