"@autoclosure in Swift is a type attribute that automatically wraps an expression in a closure without requiring explicit braces.
It's particularly useful when we want lazy evaluation — that is, delaying the execution of a value until it's actually needed.


func xyz(condition: Bool) {
    
    
}

xyz(condition: 10 > 20) // 10 > 20 evaluated BEFORE the call
xyz(condition: 10 > expensiveCheck()) // expensiveCheck() NOT run if 10 > already false

Without @autoclosure, expensive or dangerous code is always run, even if the result isn’t needed.
func assert(_ condition: @autoclosure () -> Bool, _ message: String) {
    if !condition() {
        fatalError(message)
    }
}


With @autoclosure, if the log message involves expensive string building, it's not executed unless needed.

logIfNeeded("Current time: \(expensiveComputation())")


