Touch Down
	•	Event: When the user’s finger first touches the screen.
	•	Example: UIControl.Event.touchDown
Usage:
	•	Highlight a button visually when touched.
	•	Start animations or timers.
	•	Provide immediate feedback.

Touch Up
	•	Event: When the user lifts their finger after touching the screen.
	•	Variants:
	•	touchUpInside: Finger lifted inside the bounds of the control.
	•	touchUpOutside: Finger lifted outside the bounds of the control.
	•	Usage:
	•	Trigger primary actions (touchUpInside is most commonly used for buttons).
	•	Cancel actions if touch ends outside (touchUpOutside).


====
Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)
The SRP states that a class should have only one reason to change, meaning it should have a single, well-defined responsibility. This principle is vital in iOS to prevent bloated view controllers and ensure code is easier to maintain, especially in large teams where changes to one feature shouldn’t affect unrelated logic.
imple Example: Consider a User class that manages both user data (e.g., name, ID) and authentication (e.g., login, logout). If authentication logic changes, it could inadvertently affect user data handling, violating SRP. Instead, split into:

struct User {
    let id: Int
    let name: String
}
class AuthenticationManager {
    func login(username: String, password: String) -> Bool {
        // Authentication logic, e.g., API call
        return true
    }
}
This separation ensures each class changes for one reason: User for data updates, AuthenticationManager for auth changes.


In an iOS app, a ViewController might handle both UI updates and network requests, leading to a "massive view controller" anti-pattern. To adhere to SRP, use a ViewModel to encapsulate business logic, keeping the ViewController focused on UI:


class UserViewModel {
    func fetchUsers(completion: @escaping ([User]) -> Void) {
        // Simulate network request
        let users = [User(id: 1, name: "Alice")]
        completion(users)
    }
}
class UserViewController: UIViewController {
    let viewModel = UserViewModel()
    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()
        viewModel.fetchUsers { users in
            // Update UI, e.g., reload table view
        }
    }
}


Open-Closed Principle (OCP)
The OCP suggests that software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification, allowing new functionality without altering existing code. This is particularly useful in iOS for adding features like new notification types or UI components without refactoring.

Simple Example: Imagine a Shape class with a draw() method. To add a Circle, modifying Shape violates OCP. Instead, use inheritance:

class Shape {
    func draw() {
        print("Drawing shape")
    }
}
class Circle: Shape {
    override func draw() {
        print("Drawing circle")
    }
}


Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)
The LSP states that objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of a subclass without affecting the program’s correctness. This ensures type safety and maintainability in iOS, especially for polymorphic behavior.


In an iOS app, a PaymentProcessor with processPayment() should allow subclasses like CreditCardPaymentProcessor to be used interchangeably:


protocol PaymentProcessor {
    func processPayment(amount: Decimal) -> Bool
}
class CreditCardPaymentProcessor: PaymentProcessor {
    func processPayment(amount: Decimal) -> Bool {
        print("Processing credit card payment for \(amount)")
        return true
    }
}
class PayPalPaymentProcessor: PaymentProcessor {
    func processPayment(amount: Decimal) -> Bool {
        print("Processing PayPal payment for \(amount)")
        return true
    }
}


Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)
The ISP suggests that clients should not depend on interfaces they don’t use, promoting smaller, focused protocols. This is crucial in iOS for modular, reusable code, especially with protocols like 

In an iOS app, a DatabaseManager protocol with CRUD methods forces read-only components to implement write methods. Split:
protocol DatabaseReader {
    func readData() -> Data
}

protocol DatabaseWriter {
    func writeData(data: Data)
}

class SQLiteManager: DatabaseReader, DatabaseWriter {
    func readData() -> Data {
        print("Reading data")
        return Data()
    }
    
    func writeData(data: Data) {
        print("Writing data")
    }
}

class ReadOnlyDatabase: DatabaseReader {
    func readData() -> Data {
        print("Reading from read-only source")
        return Data()
    }
}

Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)
The DIP states that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions. This is key in iOS for decoupling, especially in MVVM or dependency injection.
protocol LightProtocol {
    func turnOn()
    func turnOff()
}

class Light: LightProtocol {
    func turnOn() {}
    func turnOff() {}
}

class Switch {
    let light: LightProtocol
    
    init(light: LightProtocol) {
        self.light = light
    }
    
    func toggle() {
        if /* condition */ {
            light.turnOn()
        } else {
            light.turnOff()
        }
    }
}

protocol NetworkServiceProtocol {
    func fetchData(completion: @escaping (Data?) -> Void)
}
class NetworkService: NetworkServiceProtocol {
    func fetchData(completion: @escaping (Data?) -> Void) {
        // Network logic
    }
}
class ViewController {
    let networkService: NetworkServiceProtocol
    init(networkService: NetworkServiceProtocol) {
        self.networkService = networkService
    }
    func loadData() {
        networkService.fetchData { data in
            // Handle data
        }
    }
}


========
	•	The Builder pattern is a creational design method that lets you construct complex objects step by step.  
	•	Instead of using a single initializer with many parameters, a builder class provides methods to set each property incrementally, returning the final object via a build() method
	•	
	•	 It's useful for creating objects with many optional parameters, like in iOS UI components.
	•	


class URLRequestBuilder {
    private var url: URL?
    private var method: String = "GET"
    private var headers: [String: String] = [:]
    private var body: Data?

    func setURL(_ url: URL) -> URLRequestBuilder {
        self.url = url
        return self
    }

    func setMethod(_ method: String) -> URLRequestBuilder {
        self.method = method
        return self
    }

    func setHeader(_ key: String, value: String) -> URLRequestBuilder {
        headers[key] = value
        return self
    }

    func setBody(_ body: Data) -> URLRequestBuilder {
        self.body = body
        return self
    }

    func build() -> URLRequest? {
        guard let url = url else { return nil }
        var request = URLRequest(url: url)
        request.httpMethod = method
        for (key, value) in headers {
            request.addValue(value, forHTTPHeaderField: key)
        }
        request.httpBody = body
        return request
    }
}

// Usage
let builder = URLRequestBuilder()
    .setURL(URL(string: "https://api.example.com/data")!)
    .setMethod("POST")
    .setHeader("Content-Type", value: "application/json")
    .setBody(Data("{\"key\":\"value\"}".utf8))

if let request = builder.build() {
    URLSession.shared.dataTask(with: request) { data, response, error in
        // Handle response
    }.resume()
}


